      "text": "Write $n = 4a + 3$ and $n = 5b + 2$ over and over and we are looking at common values of $a$ and $b$ until $n > 10$. In other words, we are solving and eliminating $5b + 2\\mod 4$. We want $b = a + 1$, as then $5a + 7$ will have only 2 as its remainder.\n\nThen we have $5a + 7 > 10$. This means $a \\geq 1$. Next we try and find the smallest $a > 0$, to get the smallest $n$ greater than 10. This will be solved to be $5a + 7 = 12$, so $a = 1$ and $n = 12$\n\n\\boxed{12}. To determine the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 and satisfies the given conditions, we start by setting up the congruences based on the problem description.\n\n1. When Ray climbs 4 steps at a time, there are 3 steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n   \\]\n   This means:\n   \\[\n   n = 4a + 3\n   \\]\n   for some integer \\( a \\).\n\n2. When Ray climbs 5 steps at a time, there are 2 steps left at the top. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   This means:\n   \\[\n   n = 5b + 2\n   \\]\n   for some integer \\( b \\).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n > 10 \\) that satisfies both congruences. Therefore, we are looking for the smallest \\( n \\) such that:\n\\[\nn = 4a + 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n = 5b + 2\n\\]\n\n3. We write down these expressions and solve for \\( n \\):\n\\[\n4a + 3 = 5b + 2\n\\]\n\n4. Rearranging the equation to find a common form:\n\\[\n4a + 3 - 5b = 2\n\\]\n\\[\n4a - 5b = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n4a = 5b - 1\n\\]\n\n5. Since the left-hand side must be divisible by 4 and the right-hand side must be divisible by 5, we seek values of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) that satisfy these conditions. We need to find small \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) by checking moduli close to our requirements:\n\nLet's check for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) starting from 1:\n   \\[\n   4a - 5b = -1\n   \\]\n\n- For \\( a = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   4(1) - 5b = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4 - 5b = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -5b = -5\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = 1\n   \\]\n\nThis provides us:\n   \\[\n   n = 4(1) + 3 = 7\n   \\]\n   but \\( n = 7 \\leq 10 \\).\n\n- For \\( a = 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   4(2) - 5b = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8 - 5b = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -5b = -9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{9}{5} \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n   \\]\n\n- For \\( a = 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   4(3) - 5b = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   12 - 5b = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -5b = -13\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{13}{5} \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n   \\]\n\n- For \\( b = 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   4(1) + 3 = 7\n   \\]\n   but \\( n = 7 \\leq 10 \\).\n\n- For \\( a = 0 \\):\n   This will give \\( n = 3 \\), not greater than 10.\n\nSo, we check for greater values of \\( a \\), \\( b \\):\n   \\[\n   n = 4(2) + 3 = 11\n   \\]\nThis fits \\( n = 11 \\). Next, we need to check step-by-step larger scenarios. Let's verify scenarios for \\( a \\geq 3 \\):\n\n6 and 7 do not fit:\n   \\[\n   n = 4(3) + 3 = 15,  4(4) + 3 = 19 ;\n   19 < 30 \\\\\n   4(5) + 3 = 23 ;\n   23 < 30 \\\\\n   4(6) + 3 = 27 ;\n   27 < 30;\n   \\]\n\nSo, this would continue on to higher numbers and no suitable \\( n \\) exist three down.\n\nHence, We compute \\( \\boxed{12} \\) verification:\nCheck 12 only fits for larger numbers.\n\n\\boxed{12}",
