      "text": "Let $n=4a+3$ and $n=5b+2$. Hence, we can write,\n$b=\\left(\\frac{n-2}{5}\\right)$ and $a=\\left(\\frac{n-3}{4}\\right)$.\nWe want to find the smallest possible value of $n$ that is greater than 10.\nWe can use the following strategy to solve:\n1) List some possible values of $n$ that are congruent to $3$ modulo $4$.\n2) List some possible values of $n$ that are congruent to $2$ modulo $5$.\n3) Use pairwise comparison to find the smallest possible value of $n$.\n1) The values congruent to $3$ module $4$ are: $3, 7, 11, 15, 19, ...$ which can be written as $4a+3$.\n2) The values congruent to $2$ modulo $5$ are: $2, 7, 12, 17, 22, ...$ which can be written as $5b+2$.\nWe want to find the smallest possible value of $n$ that is greater than $10$. We can do this by doing pairwise comparison.\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\\boxed{31}",
